    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also worried about the subgrade. We had some issues with compaction during the initial prep for this section. How much of a role does subgrade instability play in longitudinal cracking compared to the binder issues you mentioned? I'm trying to figure out if we should be looking at the mix design or the ground underneath it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subgrade instability, especially if compaction was lacking, plays a massive role, often more critical than just the binder's viscosity alone. A weak subgrade is essentially a foundation that can't properly support the pavement structure, which is like building a tower on shifting sand. When the subgrade deforms under traffic loads, it induces underlying stresses that manifest as cracks on the surface, whether they run longitudinally or otherwise. You need a stable base, or the pavement will fail prematurely, no matter how perfect your mix design is.",
      "source": "target",
